Potential Flow Lab @PROJECT_VERSION@
===================================

Interactive 2D potential-flow simulator (ME semester project).

HOW TO RUN
  Double-click PotentialFlowLab.exe. Nothing needs to be installed.

REQUIREMENTS
  - Windows 10 or 11, 64-bit.
  - A graphics driver that supports OpenGL 3.3 (any GPU from roughly 2010 onward
    with up-to-date drivers). Virtual machines and Remote Desktop sessions often
    do not provide OpenGL 3.3; run the program directly on the computer instead.

IF WINDOWS SHOWS "Windows protected your PC"
  The program is not code-signed. Click "More info", then "Run anyway".

CONTROLS
  - Left toolbar: click an icon to add a uniform flow, source/sink, vortex,
    doublet, sector flow or symmetry wall, a source-panel body (ellipse,
    rectangle or NACA 4-digit airfoil) or a vortex-sheet thin airfoil (flat
    plate, NACA mean line or circular arc); enter its parameters and press
    Apply. The pencil tool draws your own body outline. Below them, the dye
    rake tool: drag a line across the plot to release dye.
  - Plot: scroll to zoom, drag to pan. Hover an element to see it, double-click
    it to edit it, drag it to move it. Click a stagnation point to show its
    dividing streamlines.
  - Edit menu: Undo (Ctrl+Z) and Redo (Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z).
  - Examples menu: ready-made scenes (cylinder, lifting cylinder, Rankine
    oval and half-body, source near a wall, corner flow, source-panel bodies,
    thin airfoils, ...). One click
    loads the scene; Save stores your own copy.
  - Elements menu: edit or delete any element.
  - File menu: New, Open, Save, Save As (scene files are JSON), Clear All,
    Import airfoil coordinates (.dat, Selig or Lednicer layout), and exports: PNG image of the plot, CSV field data, CSV streamlines,
    CSV surface Cp, and Record animation (MP4 video or animated GIF).
  - View menu: the Parameters panel (Ctrl+P; live sliders for every element
    and a parameter sweep with CSV export), streamline density, arrows, wall
    images, equipotential lines, a |V| or Cp background, surface pressure
    (Cp on the bodies, lift and drag; on a thin airfoil, its loading against
    thin-airfoil theory), stagnation points, panel strengths (λ on the panels,
    γ along an airfoil), tracer particles.
  - Verify menu (F5): checks that compare the program with exact results,
    each with its error against its tolerance.
  - Help menu: the user guide (F1), sign conventions, keyboard shortcuts and
    About (version and graphics card, useful for problem reports).
  - A scene file can also be dragged onto PotentialFlowLab.exe to open it.
